    exec: SandboxCommandRunner | undefined,
  ): Promise<{ status: "pass" | "fail" | "inconclusive"; reason: string }>;
  /**
   * Host-side TEST-CERT gate — a 1:1 mirror of `checkLintCert`. The implementer
   * certifies `SANDCASTLE-TEST: pass` in the commit body once the suite passes;
   * the reviewer verifies by running it. This host check only confirms the cert
   * is present on a code-bearing diff and returns "missing" when it isn't —
   * `shipAfterMigrations` quarantines for human triage. Required like its
   * sibling gate; the dormant cases are decided inside `classifyTestCert`, not
   * by an absent method.
   */
  checkTestCert(
    repoRoot: string,
    preSha: string,
    postSha: string,
  ): Promise<{ status: "pass" | "missing" | "dormant" }>;
  /**
   * Test-VERIFICATION gate — the deterministic backstop behind `checkTestCert`.
   * That gate only greps the commit body for `SANDCASTLE-TEST: pass`, so "the
   * tests pass" has always been the agent's unverified self-report, and agents
   * have shipped broken work with the cert present. This method RE-RUNS the
   * tests and reports what it actually observed, so the claim can be compared
   * against ground truth.
   *
   * The run happens INSIDE the sandbox, via `exec`. That is not an
   * implementation detail: the per-issue worktree's `node_modules` is installed
   * by the Linux container, so the same command on a macOS host dies on
   * `Cannot find module @rollup/rollup-darwin-arm64` before a single test
   * executes. A host-side runner therefore reported inconclusive on every Mac
   * and verified nothing. `exec` is optional on the handle — when a sandbox
   * cannot offer it (a legacy test double), the gate reports inconclusive.
   *
   * `worktreePath` is the per-issue worktree (`SandboxHandle.worktreePath`),
   * used ONLY to resolve scope: it is read from the host to parse the workspace
   * manifests, which are plain text and identical through the bind mount.
   * Nothing is executed there. `changedFiles` is the ship-time diff's paths,
   * used to scope the run to the owning workspace packages; a whole-repo run is
   * never issued (a bare `pnpm test` on a contended host once stalled 79
   * minutes and false-quarantined an unrelated issue).
   *
   * FOUR statuses, and the third is the load-bearing one:
   *   - `"pass"`         — every in-scope package's suite ran and was green.
   *   - `"fail"`         — a suite RAN and reported failure. Only this blocks.
   *   - `"inconclusive"` — the suite could not RUN (toolchain unresolvable,
   *       Postgres/browser unreachable, timed out, workspace unparseable).
   *       A test that could not run is NOT a test that failed, so this is
   *       recorded and surfaced but must never quarantine — otherwise a broken
   *       environment turns the gate into a random ticket-killer.
   *   - `"dormant"`      — nothing to verify: the cert gate was itself dormant,
   *       or the diff touched no package that defines a `test` script.
   */
